// A `!` on a module const asks for real storage: a writable global that grows like
// any other array. Any ELEMENT type qualifies — the pool emits a flat buffer for
// primitives and enums, an aggregate for struct / tuple / nested-array elements, and
// both carry a storage class. A permanent GC frame roots them, so a reallocated
// buffer is forwarded rather than collected.
//
// What does NOT qualify is a marker BELOW the array level. An aggregate entry emits
// its elements as separate `static const` objects, so `SPECS[0].code = 9` would
// write into `.rodata`. Keeping the element type read-only is exactly what makes the
// writable slots safe: `SPECS[0] = other` reaches a slot, the deeper write is T3070.
// So a marked element is rejected rather than silently unsound.
//
// A non-array const is rejected for a different reason: a bare struct or map isn't
// pooled at all, so there is no storage to make writable.
module "snippet"

Spec :: struct { code: i32 }

TPL:    Spec![]:  [Spec { .code = 1 }]
TPL2:   Spec![]!: [Spec { .code = 2 }]
INNER:  i32[]![]: [[3]]
ORIGIN: Spec!:    Spec { .code = 0 }

main :: fn() -> i32 = TPL[0].code + TPL2[0].code + INNER[0][0] + ORIGIN.code
